{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>With all your heart</b>. According to the opinion of the Midrash (Sifrei Va'etchanan 6), the heart mentioned here is the desiring faculty, as in תאות לבו נתתה לו (Psalms 21:3), אל תחמוד יפיה בלבבך (Proverbs 6:25). If so, <b>with all your soul</b> is the rational soul, and likewise והיתה נפש אדני צרורה בצרור החיים (I Samuel 25:29). And according to the words of our Sages, who expound in the Gemara (Berakhot 54a) \"from here: let him be killed rather than transgress,\" <b>with all your soul</b> would be an addition, since He said <b>with all your heart</b> and <b>with all your might</b>, He said also <b>with all your soul</b>. And the meaning is that you should love Him as much as your own soul, that you should give it over in love; or that the sufferings or the limbs are called half of the soul, and in the case of killing, He says <b>with all your soul</b>. And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said that <b>your soul</b> is the desiring faculty, as in כנפשך שבעך (Deuteronomy 23:25), נפש שבעה תבוס נפת (Proverbs 27:7), אל תתנני בנפש צרי (Psalms 27:12); <b>with all your heart</b> is the faculty of knowledge, and it is a designation for the rational spirit, for it is its first vehicle, and similar to it is חכם לב יקח מצות (Proverbs 10:8). And his interpretation is close to the explanation of <b>with all your heart</b> from what is said, והיו ההדברים האלה וגו' על לבבך." "And the meaning of <b>and with all your might</b> is: with much, much. And the sense is: love Him greatly, greatly. According to the opinion of our Sages (Berakhot 54a), it means <b>with all your money</b>, and money is called <b>מאוד</b> (might) because it designates it by abundance, just as it calls it <b>המון</b> (multitude), as in טוב מעט לצדיק מהמון רשעים רבים (Psalms 37:16), והנם ככל המון ישראל (II Kings 7:13); and it is called <b>חיל</b> (wealth), as in ישאו על כתף עירים חילם (Isaiah 30:6), חילך כל אוצרותיך (Jeremiah 17:3), את החיל הזה (Deuteronomy 8:17). He says: with all the abundance of your money. And similar to it is אשר שב אל ה' בכל לבבו ובכל נפשו ובכל מאדו (II Kings 23:25), because of his devotion in the matter of uprooting idolatry and making the Passover. And the matter of love, our Sages explained it, and what is explicit in their words is what they said in the Sifrei (Ekev): perhaps you will say, I am learning Torah in order to be called wise, in order to sit in the academy, in order to lengthen my days, in order to merit the World to Come — Scripture says, <b>to love</b>, etc. And that which Moses said to them always, למען ייטב לך (Deuteronomy 6:18) and למען תאריך ימים (Deuteronomy 4:40) and למען תחיו ובאתם וירשתם את הארץ (Deuteronomy 4:1), he warns them about fear by way of admonitions, to say that if they sin they will not inherit the Land or will not lengthen their days upon it; or that the pious one must be both loving and fearing, as they said (Yerushalmi Berakhot 9:5): act out of love and act out of fear; act out of love, for a lover does not hate; and act out of fear, for if you come to kick, do not kick. And this has already been explained in the words of the Sages."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The Gemara citation (ברכות נד) refers to Berakhot 54a; folio number given in Hebrew numerals without ע\"א/ב designation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:46:21.273377091Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}